Finally I reach the cash register with my new clothes.
The line is short, and it is quickly my turn.
I put the green jacket and the blue shirt on the counter.
The shop assistant smiles kindly at me.
"Did you find the right sizes?" she asks.
"Yes, I think so," I answer.
I have, after all, only tried on the jacket, not the shirt.
She scans the clothes and looks at the screen.
"That will be five hundred and ninety kroner, please," she says.
I pay with my card.
"Do you want the receipt with you?" she asks afterwards.
"Yes please, I would like that," I say.
She prints the receipt and hands it to me.
"Remember that you can exchange the clothes for thirty days," the shop assistant says.
"Thank you very much," I say and accept the paper.
I fold the little receipt very carefully.
Then I put it safely down in my wallet.
It is important to keep it if the shirt is too small.
Then I can indeed come back and get a bigger size.
I take my bags and walk out into the mild spring weather again.
